  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The current record stands at 0.38085 for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant. The provided solution achieves 0.381277, so there exists room for improvement. \n   The approach involves using a multi-start simulated annealing method inspired by genetic algorithms, specifically employing a mutation and crossover strategy similar to differential evolution (DE) but with local search. \n   We consider functions with multiple \"humps\" which are known to perform better than smooth or symmetric functions in such optimization problems. The algorithm will systematically modify parent solutions by perturbing segments and mirroring to maintain the integral constraint. \n   The search will prioritize configurations that yield lower C5 values, with a cooling schedule to prevent early convergence. This method is chosen to address the non-convex nature of the optimization problem and to escape local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   We represent the function as a step function with n_points samples over [0,2], satisfying:\n        h(x) \u2208 [0,1] \u2200x \u2208 [0,2]\n        \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n   For given n_points, we maintain feasibility by adjusting shifted segment sums to satisfy the integral constraint. The objective is to minimize:\n        C5 = max_{k} \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   Indicating valid shifts k, the integral is approximated in the discrete domain via correlation. The solution vector h must satisfy:\n        sum(h) * (2/n_points) = 1  \u2192 sum(h) = n_points/2\n   We use a mutation operator to introduce diversity and a crossover operator to explore new combinations. A cooling schedule with adaptive temperature reduction ensures thorough exploration. \n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Parameter initialization:\n        n_lead = n_points//2\n        n_full = 2*n_lead   # Symmetric representation\n        dx = 2.0 / n_full\n\n   - Objective function:\n        For candidate vector h (n_points samples):\n          h_full = np.array(h)   # n_points samples\n          h_rotate = np.concatenate([h, h])[::-1]  # Symmetric extension\n          corr_full = np.correlate(h_rotate, 1-h_rotate, mode='full')\n          C5_value = np.max(corr_full) * (dx if n_full<n_points else 1/(n_points))\n\n   - Candidate generation (evolution):\n        While time_remaining:\n          For each stored parent (with cooling factor):\n            Create two mutations:\n                Mutation 1: Uniform crossover between two parents\n                Mutation 2: Local perturbation of a random segment\n            Calculate C5 for both mutated candidates\n            Accept mutations if the new solution improves the best known or by Metropolis criterion\n\n        Repair integral constraint by re-normalizing and clamping:\n          total_sum = np.sum(segment)\n          normalized = segment * (1/total_sum) * (n_points/2)\n          clamped = np.clip(normalized, 0, 1)\n          adjusted = clamped * (total_sum/(np.sum(clamped)))  # Maintain integral\n          final_h = np.concatenate([adjusted[:n_lead], adjusted[-n_lead:]])\n\n   - Archiving/pareto solutions: \n        Track the lowest C5 found with valid integral and [0,1] values.\n\n   - Termination:\n        Break when time limit (budget_s) is reached or local minima are found.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   - Hyper-parameters: population size=100, mutation rate=0.9, crossover fraction=0.9\n   - Start from provided parent and 3 other constructed initial shapes\n   - Variable segment length and mutation intensity based on cooling schedule\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Estimated time:  Time per candidate is O(n_points) for convolution operations\n   - We limit total evaluations to max(n_points/10, budget_s*10) / dx_length\n   - Fallback: If interrupted early, return best solution stored in archive\n   - C5 evaluation checks: max correlation value, strict bounds satisfaction\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Final candidate must: \n        (1) Be non-empty array\n        (2) Sum exactly np.n_points/2 (within 1e-5)\n        (3) Values between 0 and 1\n        (4) C5 calculated via np.correlate(..., mode='full')*(dx)\n   - Return (np.concatenate([candidate, candidate[::-1]]), best_C5, n_points)",
